  <HOMEWORK title="Bring-Up 4: The clock recovery never actually catches up"
            prompt="FFE and channel are sorted. Bring up the receiver's clock recovery next, before touching anything else in the receiver (DFE is deliberately left off) - a stable recovered clock has to come first. These two boards each run their own independent reference oscillator rather than sharing one, so expect a real, nonzero ppm offset between TX and RX for the CDR to track out."
            hint="Check the receiver editor's reported CDR frequency offset (ppm) against where it needs to end up. Is it actually converging toward the real offset, or stuck nowhere near it?"
            explanation="The CDR's frequency-tracking (integral) gain is set to 0.5 uUI - fine if these boards shared one reference clock and only had a few ppm of local drift to chase, but these boards each have an independent oscillator and the real offset here is 3000 ppm. With the integral gain this low the loop never gets anywhere near the real offset (it settles hundreds of ppm off, not the needed -3000) - roughly a third of compared bits come back wrong, a real link failure even on runs where the CDR's own &quot;locked&quot; flag still happens to read true (the frequency error, not the lock flag, is the number to trust here). Turning the CDR's integral step back up into its normal range (tens of uUI) lets it close that gap and lock cleanly, with nothing about the channel or TX changed. The real-world version of this mistake: sizing a CDR's frequency-loop bandwidth for the wrong clocking architecture - synchronous (one shared reference) versus asynchronous (independent oscillators, a real ppm offset that has to be tracked, not just a little local jitter)."/>
</LABCHAIN>
